From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 16/22] dndcp: Use the native Wayland drag for host to guest
 drags

Where waylandDrag/ has connected, DnDUIX11 starts a host to guest drag
as a native Wayland drag and falls back to XDND otherwise.
LocalSetContentProvider() now collects the formats once, and both drag
sources offer the same MIME types and bytes. On the first motion the
plugin arms the overlays, hides the detection window, and presses
through uinput. FakeMouse_Button(), new here, skips the 100 ms pause
that FakeMouse_Click() takes for GTK's drag detection. The host's RPC
messages wait behind any sleep, and the user can let go of a quick drag
in that time.

If a drag cannot start from the press, the button is released and the
detection window is mapped again for the XDND drag, which then waits for
the window manager to adopt the window before it presses. A failure of
WaylandDrag_Init() for a reason other than a missing global, such as a
compositor that has not yet announced its outputs, is retried at the
start of the next host drag.

Send the host drag feedback before starting the drag rather than after.
The host chooses between a drop and a cancel from the feedback it has
when the user lets go, and the user can let go of a quick drag while the
overlay is still being mapped.

OnSrcDrop() must not show, raise or warp over the Xwayland detection
window during a native drag. Even after the target has accepted, any of
those makes the compositor cancel the drag instead of dropping. It moves
the pointer and releases the button through uinput only.

Read the Wayland connection between the host's motions too. The motions
come in bursts that the RPC channel handles in one go. When a target
asks for the offer's contents on entering, and the pointer has left it
again before they arrive, GTK reports a failed transfer, and libfm 1.4.1
then dereferences NULL.

A Wayland drag emits none of the GtkDragSource signals, so
OnWaylandDragDone() releases the faked press when the drag ends without
a host drop. Each native drag carries a generation number, so that the
end of an abandoned drag is not taken for the end of the next one. A new
host drag, a reset or a host cancel ends a native drag that is still
running.

In a build without Wayland support waylandDrag/waylandDragStub.c
provides the functions, and a native drag is never available.
